/* ===== Pathology 3: Haemodynamic disorders ===== */
A("path3",0,{r:R_PATH+" — Haemodynamic disorders",
s:["Oedema is extra fluid collecting in the tissue spaces.","Like a leaky garden pipe: water escapes when pressure is high, the pipe is weak, or the drain is blocked.",["Causes: high venous pressure, low protein in blood, blocked lymphatics, salt retention, leaky vessels.","Transudate (low protein) vs exudate (high protein).","Cardiac oedema starts in the feet; renal oedema starts around the eyes.","Pulmonary oedema is dangerous: fluid in air sacs."],"Starling forces: push out (hydrostatic) vs pull in (oncotic)."],
sh:"Oedema is abnormal accumulation of fluid in interstitial tissue spaces or body cavities. Mechanisms (Starling forces): increased hydrostatic pressure (CHF, venous obstruction), decreased plasma oncotic pressure (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition), lymphatic obstruction (filariasis, tumour), sodium and water retention (renal failure, RAAS), and increased permeability (inflammation). Cardiac oedema is dependent and pitting; renal oedema is periorbital; pulmonary oedema causes heavy wet lungs with frothy fluid.",
m:[["Definition","Oedema: abnormal increase of fluid in the interstitial tissue spaces or body cavities (hydrothorax, ascites, anasarca when generalised)."],
["Pathogenesis",["↑ Hydrostatic pressure: CHF, constrictive pericarditis, DVT, cirrhosis (portal)","↓ Plasma oncotic pressure: nephrotic syndrome, liver cirrhosis, protein malnutrition","Lymphatic obstruction: filariasis, tumour, post-surgery/radiation (lymphoedema)","Na⁺ and water retention: renal failure, RAAS activation in heart failure","↑ Vascular permeability: inflammation, burns, allergy (exudate)"]],
["Transudate vs exudate",[["Feature","Transudate","Exudate"],["Cause","Hydrostatic/oncotic imbalance","Inflammation, ↑ permeability"],["Protein","< 3 g/dL","> 3 g/dL"],["Specific gravity","< 1.012","> 1.020"],["Cells","Few","Many (neutrophils etc.)"],["Clots","No","Yes (fibrinogen)"]]],
["Cardiac vs pulmonary oedema",["Cardiac (right heart failure): ↑ venous pressure + RAAS; dependent pitting oedema of legs, sacrum","Pulmonary (left heart failure, ARDS): fluid first in interstitium then alveoli; lungs heavy, wet, frothy blood-tinged fluid; heart failure cells in chronic cases"]]],
l:{i:"Fluid exchange across capillaries is governed by Starling forces: hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out and plasma oncotic pressure (mainly albumin) pulls it back; lymphatics drain the excess.",
x:[["Morphology",["Subcutaneous: pitting on pressure","Brain: swollen gyri, narrowed sulci, risk of herniation","Lung: weight 2–3 times normal, frothy fluid in alveoli"]],["Clinical significance",["Pulmonary oedema impairs gas exchange — life-threatening","Cerebral oedema → herniation","Subcutaneous oedema impairs wound healing"]]],
f:"↑ Hydrostatic P  |  ↓ Oncotic P  |  Lymph block  |  Na⁺ retention  |  ↑ Permeability\n                 ↓\nFluid out of capillaries > lymph drainage → OEDEMA",
c:"Oedema reflects disturbed Starling balance; identifying the mechanism guides treatment."}});

A("path3",1,{r:R_PATH+" — Hyperaemia & congestion",
s:["Chronic venous congestion (CVC) is long-standing back-up of blood in an organ because veins cannot drain it.","Like a traffic jam behind a blocked road: cars (blood) pile up and the area gets damaged.",["Lung CVC: left heart failure → heart failure cells.","Liver CVC: right heart failure → nutmeg liver.","Spleen CVC: portal hypertension → big firm spleen with Gamna-Gandy bodies."],"Lung → Left heart; Liver → Right heart; Spleen → portal."],
sh:"Chronic venous congestion is passive engorgement of an organ with venous blood due to impaired outflow. CVC lung (left heart failure, mitral stenosis): thick fibrotic septa, haemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells), brown induration. CVC liver (right heart failure): nutmeg liver with congested dark centrilobular zones and pale periportal areas; centrilobular necrosis and cardiac sclerosis. CVC spleen (portal hypertension): congestive splenomegaly, fibrosis and Gamna-Gandy bodies.",
m:[["Definition","Congestion: passive increase in blood in a tissue due to impaired venous outflow; chronic form leads to hypoxia, haemorrhage and fibrosis."],
["CVC lung",["Cause: left ventricular failure, mitral stenosis","Gross: heavy, firm, brown ('brown induration')","Micro: dilated congested alveolar capillaries, thickened fibrotic septa, haemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells), intra-alveolar oedema"]],
["CVC liver",["Cause: right heart failure, tricuspid disease, Budd-Chiari","Gross: enlarged, tense, cut surface 'nutmeg' — dark red centrilobular areas with pale yellow periphery","Micro: centrilobular sinusoidal congestion, haemorrhage and necrosis; fatty change in periphery; long-standing → cardiac sclerosis (fibrosis)"]],
["CVC spleen",["Cause: portal hypertension (cirrhosis), right heart failure","Gross: enlarged (congestive splenomegaly), firm, capsule thickened","Micro: dilated red pulp sinusoids, fibrosis, Gamna-Gandy bodies (fibrosis + haemosiderin + calcium)"]]],
l:{i:"Chronic congestion causes hypoxia of the congested tissue, rupture of small vessels with haemorrhage, haemosiderin deposition and eventual fibrosis.",x:[["Clinical correlation",["Lung: dyspnoea, orthopnoea, haemoptysis","Liver: hepatomegaly, mild jaundice","Spleen: hypersplenism, thrombocytopenia"]]],f:"Left HF → pulmonary veins ↑ → CVC LUNG (heart failure cells)\nRight HF → hepatic veins ↑ → CVC LIVER (nutmeg)\nPortal HTN → splenic vein ↑ → CVC SPLEEN (Gamna-Gandy)",c:"Each organ's pattern of CVC points to the failing side of the heart or the portal circulation."}});

A("path3",2,{r:R_PATH+" — Shock",
s:["Shock is failure of the circulation to supply enough blood and oxygen to tissues.","Like a city where the water pump fails or pipes burst: every house runs dry.",["Types: hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic.","Stage 1: body compensates (fast pulse, vasoconstriction).","Stage 2: compensation fails, acidosis.","Stage 3: irreversible organ damage.","Organs hit: lungs (ARDS), kidneys (ATN), brain, gut, heart."],"Shock = low perfusion → hypoxia → cell injury."],
sh:"Shock is systemic hypoperfusion due to reduced cardiac output or reduced effective circulating blood volume, causing impaired tissue perfusion and cellular hypoxia. Types: hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, septic (distributive), anaphylactic, neurogenic. Stages: non-progressive (compensatory: sympathetic, RAAS, ADH), progressive (anaerobic metabolism, lactic acidosis, vasodilatation), irreversible (lysosomal enzyme release, organ failure). Morphology: ARDS (shock lung), ATN, adrenal haemorrhage, GI mucosal haemorrhage, centrilobular hepatic necrosis, DIC.",
m:[["Definition","Shock: a state of circulatory failure that impairs tissue perfusion and leads to cellular hypoxia."],
["Classification",[["Type","Mechanism","Examples"],["Hypovolaemic","↓ Blood volume","Haemorrhage, burns, vomiting, diarrhoea"],["Cardiogenic","Pump failure","MI, arrhythmia, tamponade, PE"],["Septic","Vasodilatation + leak (PAMPs, cytokines)","Gram-negative/positive sepsis"],["Anaphylactic","IgE-mediated vasodilatation","Drug, insect sting"],["Neurogenic","Loss of vascular tone","Spinal cord injury, anaesthesia"]]],
["Pathogenesis of septic shock",["Microbial products (LPS) bind TLRs → TNF, IL-1, IL-6","Endothelial activation, vasodilatation (NO), increased permeability","Procoagulant state → DIC","Metabolic: insulin resistance, hyperglycaemia; adrenal insufficiency","Immune suppression later"]],
["Stages",["Non-progressive: reflex compensation maintains perfusion of vital organs (tachycardia, peripheral vasoconstriction, renal fluid conservation)","Progressive: tissue hypoperfusion, anaerobic glycolysis, lactic acidosis, arteriolar dilatation, pooling","Irreversible: cellular and tissue injury so severe that survival is impossible even if haemodynamics are corrected"]]],
l:{i:"Shock is the final common pathway for many lethal events. The underlying problem in all types is inadequate tissue perfusion.",
x:[["Morphology",["Brain: ischaemic encephalopathy","Heart: subendocardial haemorrhage, contraction band necrosis","Lungs: diffuse alveolar damage (ARDS) — more in septic shock","Kidneys: acute tubular necrosis","Adrenals: lipid depletion; Waterhouse-Friderichsen haemorrhage (meningococcal)","GIT: mucosal haemorrhage and necrosis","Liver: fatty change, centrilobular necrosis"]],["Clinical features","Hypotension, weak rapid pulse, tachypnoea, cool clammy skin (warm in early septic shock), oliguria, confusion, raised lactate."]],
f:"Cause (haemorrhage / MI / sepsis …) → ↓ perfusion\n→ Stage 1 compensated → Stage 2 progressive (lactic acidosis) → Stage 3 irreversible\n→ multi-organ failure (ARDS, ATN, DIC)",
c:"Early recognition during the compensated stage is crucial because the irreversible stage cannot be rescued."}});

A("path3",3,{r:R_PATH+" — Thrombosis",
s:["Thrombosis is formation of a blood clot inside a living blood vessel or heart.","Like a pile-up on a road: damage to the road, slow traffic and sticky surfaces all make a jam.",["Virchow's triad: endothelial injury, abnormal flow, hypercoagulability.","Arterial thrombi: pale, platelet-rich (heart attack, stroke).","Venous thrombi: red, fibrin-rich (deep leg veins).","Lines of Zahn show it formed in flowing blood."],"Virchow: Injury, Stasis, Hypercoagulability (ISH)."],
sh:"Thrombosis is formation of a solid mass (thrombus) from blood constituents within the intact cardiovascular system during life. Virchow's triad: endothelial injury (MI, atherosclerosis, vasculitis), abnormal blood flow (stasis in veins/atria; turbulence at bifurcations, aneurysms), hypercoagulability (inherited: factor V Leiden, prothrombin mutation; acquired: immobilisation, cancer, pregnancy, OCPs, antiphospholipid syndrome). Arterial thrombi are pale and occlusive at sites of atheroma; venous thrombi are red, long and occur in deep leg veins. Ante-mortem thrombi show lines of Zahn.",
m:[["Definition","Thrombosis: formation of a solid or semisolid mass from constituents of blood within the vascular system in a living person."],
["Pathophysiology (Virchow's triad)",["Endothelial injury: exposes collagen and tissue factor; loss of anti-thrombotic properties (PGI2, NO, thrombomodulin)","Abnormal flow: stasis and turbulence bring platelets to the wall, prevent dilution of clotting factors, promote endothelial activation","Hypercoagulability: primary (factor V Leiden, prothrombin G20210A, antithrombin/protein C/S deficiency) or secondary (immobility, MI, AF, cancer, pregnancy, OCPs, nephrotic syndrome, HIT, antiphospholipid antibodies)"]],
["Types",[["Feature","Arterial thrombus","Venous thrombus"],["Site","Coronary, cerebral, femoral arteries","Deep leg veins (90%)"],["Cause","Endothelial injury, turbulence","Stasis, hypercoagulability"],["Composition","Platelets + fibrin (pale/white)","RBCs + fibrin (red)"],["Growth","Retrograde","In direction of flow"],["Effect","Ischaemia, infarction","Oedema, embolism to lung"]]],
["Morphology",["Lines of Zahn: alternating pale platelet-fibrin and dark RBC layers (formed in flowing blood)","Attached to the wall","Mural thrombi in heart/aorta; vegetations on valves"]]],
l:{i:"Haemostasis normally balances clot formation and fibrinolysis; thrombosis is its pathological activation inside vessels.",x:[["Post-mortem clot vs thrombus",[["Feature","Ante-mortem thrombus","Post-mortem clot"],["Attachment","Attached to wall","Not attached"],["Lines of Zahn","Present","Absent"],["Appearance","Dry, friable, granular","Rubbery, gelatinous, 'chicken fat' + 'currant jelly'"]]]],
f:"Endothelial injury + abnormal flow + hypercoagulability → platelet adhesion/aggregation + coagulation → THROMBUS → propagation / embolism / dissolution / organisation",
c:"Virchow's triad explains almost all thrombosis and guides prophylaxis (mobilisation, heparin)."}});

A("path3",4,{r:R_PATH+" — Thrombosis",
s:["A thrombus can grow, break off, dissolve or get absorbed into the vessel wall.","Like a fallen tree on a river: it can block more, float away, rot, or become part of the bank with a new channel cut through.",["Propagation: grows and blocks more.","Embolisation: pieces travel.","Dissolution: clot-busting enzymes clear it.","Organisation & recanalisation: becomes scar with new channels."],"PEDO: Propagation, Embolisation, Dissolution, Organisation."],
sh:"Fates of a thrombus: (1) Propagation — accumulates more platelets and fibrin, causing occlusion. (2) Embolisation — dislodges and travels (pulmonary embolism from DVT). (3) Dissolution — fibrinolysis removes recent thrombi (basis of thrombolytic therapy, effective within hours). (4) Organisation and recanalisation — ingrowth of endothelial cells, smooth muscle and fibroblasts; new capillary channels partly restore flow. Rarely, infection produces a septic (mycotic) thrombus.",
m:[["Fates",["Propagation: thrombus enlarges toward the heart (veins) causing complete occlusion","Embolisation: fragments break off → pulmonary, systemic emboli","Dissolution: plasmin-mediated fibrinolysis, mainly in fresh thrombi; tPA/streptokinase exploit this","Organisation: granulation tissue ingrowth converts thrombus into fibrous tissue","Recanalisation: new vascular channels re-establish some flow","Propagation into infection: septic thrombus → mycotic aneurysm"]],
["Clinical consequences",["Arterial: infarction (MI, stroke)","Venous: oedema, pain, pulmonary embolism"]]],
l:{i:"After formation, a thrombus undergoes one or more changes over days to weeks.",x:[],f:"THROMBUS → propagation | embolisation | dissolution | organisation → recanalisation",c:"Dissolution is possible only in fresh thrombi, so thrombolytic drugs work best early."}});

A("path3",5,{r:R_PATH+" — Embolism",
s:["An embolus is something solid, liquid or gas that travels in the blood and blocks a vessel far away.","Like a piece of debris floating down a pipe until it gets stuck in a narrow section.",["Most emboli are broken-off thrombi.","From leg veins → lungs (pulmonary embolism).","From heart/aorta → brain, kidney, legs.","Other types: fat, air, amniotic fluid, tumour."],"Most emboli = thromboemboli; PE = from DVT."],
sh:"An embolus is a detached intravascular solid, liquid or gaseous mass carried by blood to a site distant from its origin. Types: thromboembolism (commonest; pulmonary from DVT, systemic from heart/aorta), fat embolism (long bone fractures; triad of hypoxia, neurological signs, petechial rash), air/gas embolism (neck surgery, decompression sickness), amniotic fluid embolism (DIC, high mortality), atheromatous, tumour and septic emboli. Pulmonary thromboembolism: small emboli → infarction or silent; large saddle embolus → sudden death; repeated → pulmonary hypertension.",
m:[["Definition","Embolism: occlusion of part of the vascular system by a mass carried in the circulation to a site distant from its point of origin."],
["Types",["Thromboembolism (99%): pulmonary and systemic","Fat embolism: fractures of long bones, soft tissue trauma; fat globules + platelet aggregation; symptoms 1–3 days later","Air embolism: > 100 mL of air; obstetric, chest injury, IV lines; Caisson disease (nitrogen bubbles on rapid ascent)","Amniotic fluid embolism: fetal squames, lanugo, mucin in maternal pulmonary vessels; DIC","Others: atheroma, tumour, septic, foreign body (catheter tip), bone marrow"]],
["Pulmonary thromboembolism",["Source: deep veins of legs above the knee","60–80% clinically silent (small)","Medium: pulmonary haemorrhage; infarction if circulation already compromised","Large (saddle embolus at bifurcation): acute right heart failure, sudden death","Multiple recurrent: pulmonary hypertension","Paradoxical embolism via ASD/PFO to systemic circulation"]],
["Systemic thromboembolism",["Source: mural thrombi (MI, AF, dilated LA in mitral stenosis), valvular vegetations, aortic aneurysm","Sites: lower limbs (75%), brain (10%), intestine, kidney, spleen"]]],
l:{i:"Emboli lodge in vessels too small to let them pass, causing partial or complete obstruction and often infarction.",x:[],f:"DVT → embolus → right heart → pulmonary artery → PE (silent / infarct / sudden death)\nHeart/aorta mural thrombus → systemic embolus → brain, kidney, limbs",c:"The consequences depend on embolus size, site and the collateral circulation."}});

/* ===== Pathology 4: Immunopathology ===== */
A("path4",0,{r:R_PATH+" — Diseases of immunity (transplant)",
s:["Transplant rejection is the body's immune system attacking a donated organ because it looks foreign.","Like security guards attacking a new employee who has a different ID badge (HLA).",["Hyperacute: within minutes, pre-formed antibodies.","Acute: weeks to months, T cells and antibodies; treatable.","Chronic: months to years, slow scarring of vessels.","Prevention: HLA matching and immunosuppressive drugs."],"Hyper = Hours (minutes); Acute = weeks; Chronic = years."],
sh:"Transplant rejection is immune-mediated damage to an allograft due to recognition of donor HLA antigens. Hyperacute (minutes–hours): pre-formed antibodies (ABO, HLA) → complement, thrombosis, ischaemic necrosis. Acute (days–months): cellular (CD8 CTLs, CD4 cells; tubulitis, endotheliitis) and antibody-mediated (vasculitis, C4d deposition); responds to immunosuppression. Chronic (months–years): intimal fibrosis of arteries (graft arteriosclerosis), interstitial fibrosis, atrophy. Prevention: HLA matching, cross-matching, immunosuppression.",
m:[["Mechanism","Recipient T cells recognise donor HLA directly (on donor APCs) or indirectly (presented by recipient APCs); antibodies against donor HLA also cause damage."],
["Types",[["Type","Time","Mechanism","Morphology"],["Hyperacute","Minutes–hours","Pre-formed antibodies (ABO, HLA)","Thrombosis, fibrinoid necrosis, cyanotic graft"],["Acute cellular","Days–months","CD8 CTL, CD4 TH1","Interstitial lymphocytes, tubulitis, endotheliitis"],["Acute antibody-mediated","Weeks–months","Donor-specific antibodies","Vasculitis, C4d in capillaries"],["Chronic","Months–years","T cells + antibodies, cytokines","Intimal thickening, fibrosis, atrophy"]]],
["Prevention",["ABO compatibility, HLA matching, cross-match","Immunosuppression: calcineurin inhibitors (cyclosporine, tacrolimus), mycophenolate, steroids, anti-IL-2R"]]],
l:{i:"Transplant rejection is a major barrier to organ transplantation; the main targets are the highly polymorphic HLA molecules.",x:[["Graft-versus-host disease","Donor T cells (bone marrow, liver) attack recipient tissues: skin rash, diarrhoea, jaundice."]],f:"Donor HLA → recipient T cells/antibodies → rejection: hyperacute (pre-formed Ab) → acute (T cells/Ab) → chronic (vascular fibrosis)",c:"Matching and immunosuppression reduce rejection, but chronic rejection remains the main cause of late graft loss."}});

A("path4",1,{r:R_PATH+" — Diseases of immunity (AIDS); Microbiology HIV",
s:["AIDS is the late stage of HIV infection when the immune system is destroyed.","HIV attacks the 'generals' (CD4 T cells) of the immune army; without them, ordinary germs win.",["Spread: sex, blood, mother to baby.","HIV enters CD4 cells using CCR5/CXCR4.","Years of silent infection, then AIDS when CD4 < 200.","AIDS brings TB, PCP, Candida, Cryptococcus, Kaposi sarcoma.","Diagnosis: antibody tests, viral load, CD4 count."],"HIV loves CD4; AIDS = CD4 < 200 + infections/tumours."],
sh:"AIDS is caused by HIV-1/2, a retrovirus transmitted sexually, parenterally and vertically. gp120 binds CD4 with co-receptors CCR5/CXCR4; reverse transcription and integration follow. Progressive loss of CD4 T cells (direct cytopathic effect, pyroptosis, immune killing) causes immunodeficiency. Phases: acute retroviral syndrome, clinical latency, AIDS (CD4 < 200/µL or AIDS-defining illness). Manifestations: opportunistic infections (TB, PCP, Candida, Cryptococcus, Toxoplasma, CMV), tumours (Kaposi sarcoma, NHL, cervical cancer), HIV encephalopathy. Diagnosis by ELISA/rapid tests, confirmation, viral load and CD4 count.",
m:[["Etiology & routes",["HIV-1 (most), HIV-2 (West Africa); retrovirus, Lentivirus","Sexual (commonest), parenteral (blood, needles), mother-to-child (in utero, delivery, breastfeeding)"]],
["Pathogenesis",["gp120 binds CD4 + CCR5 (macrophage-tropic) or CXCR4 (T-tropic); gp41 fusion","Reverse transcriptase → DNA; integrase → provirus","CD4 T cell loss: direct lysis, pyroptosis, CTL killing, apoptosis of uninfected cells","Macrophages and dendritic cells act as reservoirs; lymphoid tissue is main site of replication","Loss of TH1 help → opportunistic infections; B-cell polyclonal activation"]],
["Clinical stages",["Acute: 3–6 weeks, mononucleosis-like illness, high viral load","Chronic latency: years, generalised lymphadenopathy, gradual CD4 decline","AIDS: CD4 < 200/µL or AIDS-defining condition"]],
["Diagnosis",["ELISA / rapid tests (NACO three-test strategy)","Western blot (rarely now), p24 antigen, HIV RNA PCR (viral load)","CD4 count for staging and monitoring"]]],
l:{i:"AIDS was first recognised in 1981; India has one of the largest numbers of people living with HIV.",
x:[["Clinical manifestations",["Infections: TB (commonest in India), Pneumocystis pneumonia, oral/oesophageal candidiasis, cryptococcal meningitis, cerebral toxoplasmosis, CMV retinitis, MAC","Tumours: Kaposi sarcoma (HHV-8), B-cell lymphomas (EBV), cervical/anal carcinoma (HPV)","CNS: HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder, encephalopathy","Wasting syndrome, chronic diarrhoea"]],["Morphology of lymph nodes","Early follicular hyperplasia → follicular involution → lymphocyte depletion (burnt-out nodes)."]],
f:"HIV (sex/blood/vertical) → gp120–CD4 + CCR5/CXCR4 → RT → integration → replication in lymphoid tissue\n→ acute syndrome → latency (CD4 falls) → AIDS (CD4 < 200): OIs + tumours",
c:"HIV causes progressive CD4 depletion; antiretroviral therapy now converts it into a chronic manageable disease."}});

A("path4",2,{r:R_PATH+" — Hypersensitivity",
s:["Hypersensitivity is when the immune system overreacts and harms our own tissues.","Like a security alarm going off for a harmless cat and smashing the windows to chase it.",["Type I: allergy (IgE, mast cells) — minutes.","Type II: antibodies attack cells (blood transfusion reaction).","Type III: immune complexes deposit (SLE, PSGN).","Type IV: T cells (TB test, contact dermatitis) — 2–3 days."],"ACID: Allergic, Cytotoxic, Immune complex, Delayed."],
sh:"Hypersensitivity reactions are exaggerated or inappropriate immune responses causing tissue injury (Gell & Coombs). Type I (immediate): IgE on mast cells → histamine, leukotrienes; anaphylaxis, asthma, hay fever. Type II (antibody-mediated): IgG/IgM against cell-surface/matrix antigens → opsonisation, complement, ADCC, or receptor dysfunction; transfusion reactions, AIHA, Goodpasture, Graves, myasthenia. Type III (immune complex): deposition → complement, neutrophils; SLE, PSGN, serum sickness, Arthus. Type IV (cell-mediated): TH1/CD8 T cells; tuberculin reaction, contact dermatitis, granulomas.",
m:[["Classification",[["Type","Mediator","Mechanism","Examples"],["I Immediate","IgE, mast cells","Degranulation: histamine, LTs, PGs","Anaphylaxis, asthma, urticaria, hay fever"],["II Cytotoxic","IgG/IgM","Complement lysis, opsonisation, ADCC, receptor block/stimulation","Transfusion reaction, HDN, AIHA, Goodpasture, Graves, myasthenia"],["III Immune complex","Ag–Ab complexes","Deposition → complement → neutrophil injury","SLE, PSGN, serum sickness, Arthus, PAN"],["IV Delayed","TH1, CD8 T cells","Cytokines, macrophage activation, cytotoxicity","Tuberculin test, contact dermatitis, TB granuloma, type 1 DM"]]],
["Type I phases",["Sensitisation: TH2 → IL-4 → IgE binds mast cells","Immediate phase (minutes): vasodilatation, leak, bronchospasm","Late phase (2–24 h): eosinophils, neutrophils, tissue damage"]]],
l:{i:"Hypersensitivity reactions can be triggered by environmental antigens (allergy) or self antigens (autoimmunity).",x:[["Related reactions",["Arthus reaction: localised type III vasculitis at injection site","Shwartzman reaction: non-immunological endotoxin-induced reaction","Atopy: genetic predisposition to type I reactions"]]],f:"I: Ag + IgE on mast cell → histamine (minutes)\nII: Ab on cell surface → lysis\nIII: Ag–Ab complexes in vessels → inflammation\nIV: sensitised T cells → cytokines/killing (48–72 h)",c:"Identifying the type explains the timing, lesions and treatment of the disease."}});

A("path4",3,{r:R_PATH+" — Autoimmune diseases (SLE)",
s:["SLE (lupus) is an autoimmune disease where antibodies attack many organs, especially skin, joints, kidneys and blood.","The immune system forgets who is 'self' and attacks the body's own nuclear material.",["Commoner in young women.","Butterfly rash, joint pain, kidney disease, low blood counts.","ANA positive; anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are specific.","Immune complexes cause damage (type III)."],"SOAP BRAIN MD: Serositis, Oral ulcers, Arthritis, Photosensitivity, Blood, Renal, ANA, Immunologic, Neuro, Malar rash, Discoid rash."],
sh:"Systemic lupus erythematosus is a chronic multisystem autoimmune disease characterised by autoantibodies, especially antinuclear antibodies (ANA), causing injury mainly through immune complex deposition (type III) and antibodies against cells (type II). Women of reproductive age are mostly affected. Features: malar rash, photosensitivity, arthritis, serositis, glomerulonephritis (wire-loop lesions), cytopenias, CNS involvement, Libman-Sacks endocarditis. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are specific; LE cell phenomenon; low complement in active disease.",
m:[["Pathogenesis",["Genetic: HLA-DR2/DR3, complement deficiency (C2, C4, C1q)","Environmental: UV light, drugs (hydralazine, procainamide), oestrogens","Defective clearance of apoptotic cells → nuclear antigens exposed → TLR activation, type I interferon","Autoantibodies → immune complexes (type III) and cell lysis (type II)"]],
["Autoantibodies",["ANA: sensitive (>95%), not specific","Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith: specific","Anti-histone: drug-induced lupus","Antiphospholipid antibodies: thrombosis, recurrent abortion, false-positive VDRL"]],
["Organ involvement",["Skin: malar (butterfly) rash, discoid lesions","Joints: non-erosive arthritis","Kidney: lupus nephritis classes I–VI; wire-loop lesions (subendothelial deposits)","Heart: pericarditis, Libman-Sacks endocarditis","Blood: haemolytic anaemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia","CNS, serosa (pleuritis), spleen (onion-skin arteriolar fibrosis)"]]],
l:{i:"SLE is the prototype of systemic autoimmune disease.",x:[["LE cell","A neutrophil or macrophage that has engulfed the denatured nucleus of an injured cell (haematoxylin body)."]],f:"Genes + UV/drugs/hormones → defective apoptotic clearance → nuclear antigens → ANA → immune complexes → skin, joints, kidney, vessels, blood cells",c:"SLE is a type III (and II) hypersensitivity disease; anti-dsDNA and low complement track renal activity."}});

A("path4",4,{r:R_PATH+" — Amyloidosis",
s:["Amyloidosis is the deposit of abnormally folded proteins (amyloid) outside cells, which stiffens and damages organs.","Like glue hardening between bricks: the wall becomes stiff and stops working.",["Amyloid is made of different proteins in different diseases.","AL: from plasma cell light chains (myeloma).","AA: from chronic inflammation (TB, RA).","Stains with Congo red: apple-green under polarised light.","Kidney is most often affected."],"AL = Light chains; AA = Acute-phase SAA from chronic inflammation."],
sh:"Amyloidosis is extracellular deposition of misfolded proteins that aggregate into insoluble β-pleated sheet fibrils. Physical nature: non-branching fibrils 7.5–10 nm (EM), cross-β pleated sheet (X-ray crystallography). Chemical: AL (Ig light chains – myeloma, primary), AA (serum amyloid A – chronic inflammation), Aβ (Alzheimer), ATTR (transthyretin – senile, familial), β2-microglobulin (dialysis), calcitonin (medullary thyroid carcinoma). Stains: Congo red (apple-green birefringence), thioflavin T, metachromasia with crystal violet. Organs: kidney (commonest, nephrotic syndrome), liver, spleen (sago/lardaceous), heart (restrictive cardiomyopathy).",
m:[["Nature",["Physical: non-branching fibrils 7.5–10 nm wide on EM; cross-β-pleated sheet; plus P component and glycosaminoglycans","Chemical: ~95% fibril proteins (AL, AA, others), 5% P component"]],
["Classification",[["Category","Protein","Associated disease"],["Primary / immunocyte","AL (light chains, λ > κ)","Multiple myeloma, B-cell dyscrasias"],["Reactive systemic (secondary)","AA (from SAA)","TB, RA, bronchiectasis, osteomyelitis, IBD"],["Haemodialysis-associated","Aβ2-microglobulin","Long-term dialysis"],["Hereditary","ATTR (mutant transthyretin)","Familial amyloid polyneuropathy"],["Senile systemic","ATTR (normal)","Elderly heart"],["Localised/endocrine","Aβ, calcitonin, amylin","Alzheimer, medullary thyroid ca, type 2 DM"]]],
["Pathogenesis","Abnormal folding of normal proteins produced in excess (AA, AL) or of mutant proteins; failure of degradation → aggregation into fibrils."],
["Staining",["H&E: pale pink, amorphous, hyaline extracellular material","Congo red: pink-red; apple-green birefringence under polarised light","Lugol's iodine on gross specimen: mahogany brown → blue with H2SO4","Metachromasia: crystal violet/methyl violet (rose-pink)","Thioflavin T: fluorescence"]]],
l:{i:"Amyloid is not a single substance but a group of proteins sharing a common folding pattern.",
x:[["Organs involved",["Kidney (commonest, worst prognosis): glomerular deposits → nephrotic syndrome → renal failure; large pale waxy kidneys","Spleen: sago spleen (follicles; tapioca-like) or lardaceous spleen (red pulp, diffuse)","Liver: space of Disse, hepatomegaly","Heart: restrictive cardiomyopathy, arrhythmias (senile ATTR, AL)","Others: tongue (macroglossia), GIT, adrenals, nerves"]],["Diagnosis","Biopsy of abdominal fat pad, rectum or gingiva with Congo red; serum/urine immunofixation for AL."]],
f:"Excess/mutant protein (light chain, SAA, TTR…) → misfolding → β-pleated sheet fibrils → extracellular deposition → organ dysfunction (kidney, heart, liver, spleen)",
c:"Amyloidosis is a protein-misfolding disease; the type of amyloid determines the cause, organs involved and treatment."}});

/* ===== Pathology 5: Neoplasia ===== */
A("path5",0,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia",
s:["Neoplasia is new, uncontrolled growth of cells that continues even after the cause is removed.","A car with a stuck accelerator and broken brakes.",["Benign tumours stay local, grow slowly and look like normal tissue.","Malignant tumours invade and spread (metastasise).","Named by origin: -oma (benign), carcinoma (epithelial), sarcoma (mesenchymal).","Caused by mutations in genes controlling growth."],"Malignant = invades + metastasises."],
sh:"Neoplasia is abnormal, excessive, uncoordinated proliferation of cells that persists after the initiating stimulus is withdrawn. Tumours are classified as benign (well differentiated, slow, expansile, encapsulated, no metastasis) or malignant (variable differentiation/anaplasia, rapid growth, local invasion and metastasis). Nomenclature: benign epithelial — adenoma/papilloma; malignant epithelial — carcinoma; benign mesenchymal — -oma (lipoma); malignant mesenchymal — sarcoma. Characteristics compared: differentiation and anaplasia, rate of growth, local invasion, metastasis.",
m:[["Definition","Neoplasm (Willis): an abnormal mass of tissue, the growth of which exceeds and is uncoordinated with that of normal tissue, and persists in the same excessive manner after cessation of the stimuli which evoked the change."],
["Classification & nomenclature",[["Tissue","Benign","Malignant"],["Squamous epithelium","Squamous papilloma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],["Glandular epithelium","Adenoma","Adenocarcinoma"],["Fat","Lipoma","Liposarcoma"],["Bone","Osteoma","Osteosarcoma"],["Smooth muscle","Leiomyoma","Leiomyosarcoma"],["Blood vessels","Haemangioma","Angiosarcoma"],["Lymphoid","—","Lymphoma, leukaemia"],["Germ cells","Mature teratoma","Immature teratoma, seminoma"]]],
["Benign vs malignant",[["Feature","Benign","Malignant"],["Differentiation","Well differentiated","Variable; anaplasia"],["Rate of growth","Slow","Rapid, mitoses many/abnormal"],["Local invasion","Expansile, encapsulated","Infiltrative, no capsule"],["Metastasis","Absent","Present"],["Nuclei","Normal","Pleomorphic, hyperchromatic, high N:C, prominent nucleoli"],["Effect","Pressure","Destruction, cachexia, death"]]],
["Characteristics of tumours",["Differentiation & anaplasia: pleomorphism, abnormal nuclear morphology, mitoses, loss of polarity, tumour giant cells","Rate of growth: doubling time, growth fraction","Local invasion: next to metastasis, the most reliable feature of malignancy","Metastasis: lymphatic, haematogenous, seeding of body cavities"]]],
l:{i:"Cancer is the second commonest cause of death worldwide. Every tumour has two components: neoplastic parenchymal cells (determine behaviour and name) and reactive stroma (blood vessels, connective tissue).",
x:[["Exceptions in nomenclature",["Malignant tumours with benign-sounding names: melanoma, lymphoma, seminoma, mesothelioma, hepatoma","Hamartoma: disorganised mass of tissue native to the site","Choristoma: normal tissue at an abnormal site (heterotopia)"]],["Predisposing conditions",["Age, geography, environment (tobacco, alcohol, UV, infections)","Hereditary: familial retinoblastoma, BRCA, FAP, Lynch syndrome","Chronic inflammation, precursor lesions (Barrett, leukoplakia, cirrhosis)"]]],
f:"Normal cell → mutations (oncogene ↑, tumour suppressor ↓, apoptosis ↓) → clonal proliferation\n  → BENIGN (differentiated, local) | MALIGNANT (anaplasia → invasion → metastasis)",
c:"Neoplasia results from genetic damage; the behaviour of a tumour is judged by differentiation, growth, invasion and above all metastasis."}});

A("path5",1,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia (invasion and metastasis)",
s:["Metastasis is cancer spreading from where it started to distant organs.","Like seeds blowing from a weed in one garden and growing in another.",["Cells loosen from each other.","They break through the basement membrane.","Enter blood or lymph vessels.","Survive the journey, exit and grow at a new site."],"Loosen → Dissolve → Move → Enter → Travel → Exit → Grow."],
sh:"Metastasis is spread of tumour to sites physically discontinuous with the primary. Steps: (1) invasion of extracellular matrix — loosening of cells (loss of E-cadherin), degradation of ECM (MMPs, cathepsins), attachment to new ECM (laminin, fibronectin receptors), migration (autocrine motility factors); (2) vascular dissemination — intravasation, survival as tumour emboli with platelets, adhesion and extravasation, colonisation (organ tropism by chemokines/adhesion molecules). Routes: lymphatic (carcinomas), haematogenous (sarcomas, also RCC, HCC), transcoelomic (ovary, stomach).",
m:[["Invasion of ECM",["Detachment: loss of E-cadherin","Degradation: MMPs (type IV collagenase), cathepsin D, urokinase plasminogen activator","Attachment to ECM: laminin and integrin receptors","Migration: autocrine motility factors, cleavage products of ECM"]],
["Vascular dissemination & homing",["Intravasation into vessels/lymphatics","Survival: platelet aggregates protect emboli","Adhesion to endothelium, extravasation","Organ tropism: chemokine receptors (CXCR4 in breast cancer), adhesion molecules","Colonisation, angiogenesis (VEGF), dormancy possible"]],
["Routes",["Lymphatic: carcinomas → regional nodes; sentinel node","Haematogenous: sarcomas; liver (portal) and lungs (caval) commonest; RCC → renal vein, HCC → hepatic vein","Seeding of body cavities: ovarian carcinoma (peritoneum), Krukenberg tumour"]]],
l:{i:"Metastasis is the single most reliable feature of malignancy and the main cause of cancer death.",x:[],f:"Loss of E-cadherin → MMP degradation of BM → attachment (laminin) → migration → intravasation → emboli + platelets → extravasation → colonisation + angiogenesis",c:"Each step offers a target for anti-metastatic therapy."}});

A("path5",2,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia (grading and staging)",
s:["Grading tells how ugly (undifferentiated) the cancer cells look; staging tells how far the cancer has spread.","Grading is judging how wild one bad apple looks; staging is how many boxes of apples it has spoiled.",["Grade: based on differentiation and mitoses under the microscope (I–IV).","Stage: TNM — Tumour size, Nodes, Metastasis.","Staging is more important for prognosis and treatment."],"Grade = Glass slide; Stage = Spread."],
sh:"Grading is based on the degree of differentiation of tumour cells and number of mitoses (Grade I well differentiated to Grade IV anaplastic; e.g., Gleason score in prostate, Nottingham grade in breast). Staging is based on the size of the primary tumour, extent of spread to regional lymph nodes and distant metastasis, using the TNM system (AJCC/UICC) or others (Dukes, Ann Arbor, FIGO). Staging has greater clinical value than grading.",
m:[["Grading",["Based on cytological differentiation and mitotic activity","Grade I: well differentiated (> 75% differentiated)","Grade II: moderately differentiated","Grade III: poorly differentiated","Grade IV: anaplastic/undifferentiated","Examples: Gleason (prostate), Nottingham/Bloom-Richardson (breast), Fuhrman/ISUP (kidney)"]],
["Staging (TNM)",["T: size/extent of primary (T0–T4, Tis)","N: regional lymph node involvement (N0–N3)","M: distant metastasis (M0, M1)","Combined into stage groups I–IV","Other systems: Dukes (colon), Ann Arbor (lymphoma), FIGO (gynaecological), Clark/Breslow (melanoma)"]],
["Comparison",[["Feature","Grading","Staging"],["Basis","Microscopic differentiation","Anatomical extent of spread"],["Method","Histology","Clinical, imaging, surgical, pathological"],["Prognostic value","Less","More"]]]],
l:{i:"Grading and staging help predict behaviour and choose treatment.",x:[],f:"Histology → GRADE (I–IV)\nClinical/pathological spread → STAGE (TNM → I–IV)",c:"Staging is the more powerful predictor of outcome."}});

A("path5",3,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia (molecular basis)",
s:["Cancer is a genetic disease: a cell collects mutations that switch on growth genes and switch off brake genes.","A car with the accelerator stuck (oncogene) and the brakes cut (tumour suppressor loss).",["Oncogenes: RAS, MYC, HER2, BCR-ABL.","Tumour suppressors: RB, p53, APC, BRCA.","Other hits: escape from apoptosis, unlimited division (telomerase), new blood vessels, invasion.","Many steps over years."],"Hallmarks: grow, ignore stop, don't die, divide forever, feed (vessels), invade, change metabolism, hide from immunity."],
sh:"Cancer results from non-lethal genetic damage in a clone of cells. Four classes of regulatory genes are targeted: growth-promoting proto-oncogenes (RAS, MYC, ERBB2, ABL), growth-inhibiting tumour suppressor genes (RB, TP53, APC, NF1, BRCA1/2), genes regulating apoptosis (BCL-2) and DNA repair genes (mismatch repair in Lynch syndrome). Hallmarks of cancer: self-sufficiency in growth signals, insensitivity to growth inhibitors, altered metabolism (Warburg effect), evasion of apoptosis, limitless replicative potential (telomerase), sustained angiogenesis, invasion and metastasis, evasion of immunity; enabled by genomic instability and tumour-promoting inflammation.",
m:[["Principles",["Non-lethal genetic damage is central","Tumours are monoclonal","Carcinogenesis is multistep (accumulation of mutations)","Four classes of genes: proto-oncogenes, tumour suppressors, apoptosis regulators, DNA repair genes"]],
["Hallmarks of cancer",["Self-sufficiency in growth signals: RAS, HER2, MYC activation","Insensitivity to growth inhibition: RB, p53 loss; TGF-β pathway defects","Altered cellular metabolism: aerobic glycolysis (Warburg)","Evasion of apoptosis: BCL-2 ↑, p53 loss","Limitless replicative potential: telomerase","Sustained angiogenesis: VEGF","Invasion and metastasis","Evasion of immune surveillance (PD-L1)","Enablers: genomic instability, inflammation"]],
["Mechanisms of oncogene activation",["Point mutation (RAS)","Translocation (BCR-ABL in CML; MYC in Burkitt)","Gene amplification (HER2/neu in breast, N-MYC in neuroblastoma)"]]],
l:{i:"Molecular understanding of cancer underlies targeted therapy.",x:[["Targeted therapy examples",["Imatinib → BCR-ABL","Trastuzumab → HER2","EGFR inhibitors in lung adenocarcinoma","PD-1/PD-L1 inhibitors restore immune attack"]]],f:"Carcinogens / inherited mutations → oncogene activation + tumour suppressor loss + apoptosis evasion + DNA repair defects → clonal expansion → progression (more mutations) → invasion & metastasis",c:"Cancer is the result of accumulated mutations that confer the hallmarks of malignancy."}});

A("path5",4,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia (carcinogenic agents)",
s:["Chemical carcinogenesis is cancer caused by chemicals that damage DNA.","Initiation is the first spark (mutation); promotion is the fuel that makes the fire spread.",["Initiators cause permanent DNA mutations.","Promoters make mutated cells multiply but do not damage DNA.","Direct-acting chemicals need no change; indirect ones are activated by liver enzymes.","Examples: tobacco, aflatoxin, benzene, vinyl chloride."],"Initiation = Irreversible; Promotion = Proliferation (reversible)."],
sh:"Chemical carcinogenesis is a multistep process: initiation (exposure to carcinogen causing permanent DNA damage — irreversible, rapid, needs cell division) followed by promotion (non-mutagenic agents like phorbol esters, hormones, phenols induce proliferation of initiated cells — reversible) and progression. Carcinogens: direct-acting (alkylating agents like cyclophosphamide, acylating agents) and indirect-acting/procarcinogens requiring metabolic activation by cytochrome P450 (polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons in tobacco smoke, aromatic amines/azo dyes like β-naphthylamine, aflatoxin B1, nitrosamines, vinyl chloride).",
m:[["Stages",["Initiation: carcinogen → reactive electrophile → DNA adduct → mutation; irreversible, single exposure may suffice","Promotion: promoters (TPA/phorbol esters, oestrogen, saccharin) → clonal proliferation; reversible, needs repeated exposure after initiation","Progression: further mutations, malignant behaviour"]],
["Classification of carcinogens",[["Type","Examples","Cancer"],["Direct-acting alkylating","Cyclophosphamide, busulfan","Leukaemia, lymphoma"],["Polycyclic hydrocarbons","Benzpyrene (tobacco, smoked food)","Lung, oral cancers"],["Aromatic amines/azo dyes","β-naphthylamine, aniline dyes","Bladder cancer"],["Natural","Aflatoxin B1 (Aspergillus flavus)","Hepatocellular carcinoma"],["Nitrosamines","Preserved foods","Gastric cancer"],["Others","Vinyl chloride; asbestos; benzene; arsenic","Hepatic angiosarcoma; mesothelioma; AML; skin cancer"]]],
["Metabolic activation","Most carcinogens are procarcinogens converted by cytochrome P450 enzymes (CYP1A1) into ultimate carcinogens; genetic polymorphism affects risk."]],
l:{i:"Percivall Pott first linked soot to scrotal cancer in chimney sweeps (1775); experimental work established the initiation-promotion model.",x:[],f:"Procarcinogen → (CYP450) → ultimate carcinogen → DNA adduct → INITIATION (mutation)\n→ PROMOTION (proliferation by promoters) → PROGRESSION → cancer",c:"The two-stage model explains why avoiding promoters after exposure can still reduce cancer risk."}});

A("path5",5,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia (clinical aspects)",
s:["Paraneoplastic syndromes are symptoms caused by substances (often hormones) made by a tumour, not by the tumour's size or spread.","The tumour sends fake messages (hormones) that confuse distant organs.",["Hypercalcaemia: PTHrP from squamous cell lung cancer.","Cushing syndrome: ACTH from small cell lung cancer.","SIADH: ADH from small cell lung cancer.","Polycythaemia: erythropoietin from renal cell carcinoma.","May be the first sign of hidden cancer."],"Small cell = ACTH + ADH; Squamous = Calcium."],
sh:"Paraneoplastic syndromes are symptom complexes in cancer patients not explained by local or distant spread of the tumour or by hormones native to the tissue of origin. Examples: hypercalcaemia (PTHrP — squamous cell lung carcinoma, breast, kidney), Cushing syndrome (ectopic ACTH — small cell carcinoma lung), SIADH (small cell lung carcinoma), hypoglycaemia (insulin-like factors — fibrosarcoma, HCC), polycythaemia (erythropoietin — RCC, HCC, cerebellar haemangioma), Lambert-Eaton syndrome and myasthenia (small cell lung, thymoma), acanthosis nigricans (gastric carcinoma), Trousseau migratory thrombophlebitis (pancreatic, lung), clubbing/hypertrophic osteoarthropathy (lung), non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis.",
m:[["Importance",["May be the earliest manifestation of an occult cancer","Can cause significant morbidity or death","May mimic metastatic disease","Can be used as tumour markers"]],
["Common syndromes",[["Syndrome","Mechanism","Tumour"],["Hypercalcaemia","PTHrP, TGF-α","Squamous cell ca lung, breast, RCC"],["Cushing syndrome","Ectopic ACTH","Small cell ca lung, carcinoid"],["SIADH","ADH","Small cell ca lung"],["Hypoglycaemia","Insulin/IGF-II","Fibrosarcoma, HCC"],["Polycythaemia","Erythropoietin","RCC, HCC, cerebellar haemangioblastoma"],["Lambert-Eaton","Antibodies to Ca²⁺ channels","Small cell ca lung"],["Acanthosis nigricans","Growth factor (TGF-α)","Gastric carcinoma"],["Trousseau phenomenon","Mucin, procoagulants","Pancreatic, lung"],["Clubbing / HPOA","Unknown","Lung carcinoma"],["NBTE","Hypercoagulability","Adenocarcinomas"]]]],
l:{i:"Paraneoplastic syndromes occur in about 10% of cancer patients.",x:[["Tumour lysis syndrome (related)","Rapid cell death after chemotherapy releases K⁺, phosphate, uric acid → hyperkalaemia, hypocalcaemia, AKI (lymphomas, leukaemias)."]],f:"Tumour → ectopic hormone / antibodies / procoagulants → distant effects (Ca²⁺↑, Cushing, SIADH, thrombosis, neuro)",c:"Recognising a paraneoplastic syndrome can lead to early diagnosis of an underlying cancer."}});

A("path5",6,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia (laboratory diagnosis)",
s:["Cancer is confirmed by looking at cells or tissue under the microscope, helped by special stains and molecular tests.","Taking a sample from the suspicious lump and letting the pathologist read it like a fingerprint.",["Histopathology (biopsy) is the gold standard.","FNAC: quick needle sample of cells.","Frozen section: rapid diagnosis during surgery.","IHC: special antibody stains tell the tumour type.","Tumour markers, flow cytometry and molecular tests add information."],"Biopsy is boss; IHC tells the origin."],
sh:"Laboratory diagnosis of cancer: (1) Histological methods — incisional/excisional biopsy, frozen section (intraoperative), core biopsy; (2) Cytological methods — exfoliative cytology (Pap smear, sputum, urine, effusions), FNAC; (3) Immunohistochemistry (cytokeratin for carcinoma, vimentin for sarcoma, LCA/CD45 for lymphoma, S-100/HMB-45 for melanoma, ER/PR/HER2); (4) Flow cytometry (leukaemia/lymphoma immunophenotyping); (5) Molecular diagnosis — PCR, FISH, NGS for translocations, amplifications, mutations; (6) Tumour markers (AFP, CEA, PSA, CA-125, hCG); (7) Electron microscopy.",
m:[["Methods",["Histopathology: gold standard; adequate, well-fixed (10% formalin) tissue","Frozen section: rapid diagnosis in 15–20 min to guide surgery, margin assessment","Cytology: Pap smear (cervix), FNAC (breast, thyroid, lymph nodes; 22–24 G needle), fluid cytology","Immunohistochemistry: identifies cell type, origin, prognostic/predictive markers","Flow cytometry: haematological malignancies, DNA ploidy","Molecular: PCR (BCR-ABL), FISH (HER2), NGS (EGFR, KRAS)","Tumour markers: supportive, mainly for monitoring","Electron microscopy: rarely, for specific organelles"]],
["IHC markers",[["Marker","Tumour"],["Cytokeratin","Carcinoma"],["Vimentin","Sarcoma, melanoma"],["CD45 (LCA)","Lymphoma"],["S-100, HMB-45","Melanoma"],["Desmin","Muscle tumours"],["Chromogranin, synaptophysin","Neuroendocrine tumours"],["ER, PR, HER2","Breast carcinoma (therapy)"]]]],
l:{i:"Accurate diagnosis needs clinical data, proper sampling and correct fixation.",x:[],f:"Clinical suspicion → imaging → sample (FNAC / biopsy / frozen) → H&E → IHC → molecular tests → diagnosis, grade, stage, markers",c:"Combining histology with IHC and molecular tests gives diagnosis, prognosis and therapy targets."}});

A("path5",7,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia (tumour markers)",
s:["Tumour markers are substances made by tumours that can be measured in blood, urine or tissue.","Like smoke signals: they suggest a fire somewhere but don't prove where.",["AFP: liver cancer, yolk sac tumour.","CEA: colon cancer.","PSA: prostate cancer.","CA-125: ovarian cancer; CA 19-9: pancreatic cancer.","hCG: choriocarcinoma, testicular tumours.","Best for monitoring treatment and recurrence, not screening."],"AFP = Liver/Yolk; CEA = Colon; PSA = Prostate; 125 = Ovary; 19-9 = Pancreas."],
sh:"Tumour markers are tumour-derived or tumour-associated molecules detected in blood or body fluids. They support diagnosis and are mainly used to monitor response to therapy and detect recurrence; most are not specific enough for screening. Examples: hormones (hCG — choriocarcinoma, testicular tumours; calcitonin — medullary thyroid carcinoma; catecholamines/VMA — pheochromocytoma, neuroblastoma), oncofetal antigens (AFP — HCC, yolk sac tumour; CEA — colorectal, pancreatic, gastric, breast), enzymes (PSA — prostate; neuron-specific enolase — small cell lung, neuroblastoma), glycoproteins (CA-125 — ovarian; CA 19-9 — pancreatic, colon; CA 15-3 — breast), immunoglobulins (M protein — myeloma).",
m:[["Uses",["Supportive diagnosis","Monitoring response to therapy","Detecting recurrence","Prognosis","Screening only in selected settings (PSA debated)"]],
["Common markers",[["Marker","Main tumour(s)"],["AFP","Hepatocellular ca, yolk sac tumour"],["CEA","Colorectal, pancreas, stomach, breast, lung"],["hCG","Choriocarcinoma, hydatidiform mole, germ cell tumours"],["PSA","Prostate carcinoma"],["CA-125","Ovarian epithelial tumours"],["CA 19-9","Pancreas, colon, biliary"],["CA 15-3","Breast"],["Calcitonin","Medullary thyroid carcinoma"],["Catecholamines/VMA","Pheochromocytoma, neuroblastoma"],["NSE","Small cell lung ca, neuroblastoma"],["M protein / Bence Jones","Multiple myeloma"],["LDH","Lymphoma, germ cell tumours"]]]],
l:{i:"An ideal marker would be specific and sensitive for one cancer; none is perfect, so markers are interpreted with clinical and histological findings.",x:[["Limitations","False positives (CEA in smokers, IBD; AFP in pregnancy, hepatitis; PSA in BPH, prostatitis)."]],f:"Tumour → marker in blood → baseline → treatment → falling level (response) → rising again (recurrence)",c:"Tumour markers are most valuable for follow-up rather than initial diagnosis."}});

A("path5",8,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia (tumour suppressor genes)",
s:["p53 and RB are 'brake' genes that stop damaged cells from dividing.","RB is the gatekeeper at the door to cell division; p53 is the guardian who checks for DNA damage and orders repair or self-destruction.",["Both copies must be lost to cause cancer (two hits).","RB on chromosome 13q14: retinoblastoma, osteosarcoma.","p53 on 17p13: mutated in over half of all cancers.","HPV proteins E6 (p53) and E7 (RB) disable them."],"RB = gatekeeper; p53 = guardian."],
sh:"RB (13q14) controls the G1→S transition: hypophosphorylated RB binds E2F and blocks transcription of S-phase genes; cyclin D–CDK4/6 phosphorylates RB to release E2F. Loss of both alleles (Knudson two-hit) causes retinoblastoma (familial: bilateral, early; sporadic: unilateral) and osteosarcoma. p53 (17p13, 'guardian of the genome') senses DNA damage and induces cell-cycle arrest (via p21), DNA repair or apoptosis (via BAX, PUMA); it is mutated in > 50% of cancers; germline mutation causes Li-Fraumeni syndrome. HPV E7 inactivates RB and E6 promotes p53 degradation.",
m:[["RB gene",["Chromosome 13q14; 'governor/gatekeeper' of the cell cycle","Hypophosphorylated RB binds E2F → cell held in G1","Growth signals → cyclin D/CDK4 phosphorylates RB → E2F free → S phase","Two-hit hypothesis (Knudson): familial retinoblastoma (1 inherited + 1 somatic hit) vs sporadic (2 somatic hits)","Associated: retinoblastoma, osteosarcoma, small cell lung ca, breast ca","Inactivated by HPV E7, SV40 large T"]],
["p53 gene",["Chromosome 17p13; 'guardian of the genome'","Activated by DNA damage, hypoxia (via ATM/ATR)","Effects: transcription of p21 (arrest in G1), GADD45 (DNA repair), BAX/PUMA (apoptosis), senescence","Mutated in > 50% of human cancers","Li-Fraumeni syndrome: germline p53 mutation → sarcomas, breast, brain, leukaemia, adrenal tumours","Inactivated by HPV E6 (degradation), MDM2 overexpression"]]],
l:{i:"Tumour suppressor genes encode proteins that inhibit cell proliferation; both alleles usually need to be inactivated.",x:[],f:"DNA damage → p53 ↑ → p21 → CDK inhibition → RB stays active (binds E2F) → G1 arrest → repair OR apoptosis\nLoss of RB/p53 → uncontrolled entry into S phase → mutations accumulate",c:"RB and p53 are the central brakes on the cell cycle; their loss is a key step in most cancers."}});

A("path5",9,{r:R_PATH+" — Neoplasia (microbial carcinogenesis)",
s:["Some viruses cause cancer by inserting genes that switch off our anti-cancer brakes or by causing long-term inflammation.","A burglar (virus) who disables the alarm system (p53, RB) so the cell keeps dividing.",["HPV: cervical, oral, anal cancer.","EBV: Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal cancer, Hodgkin.","Hepatitis B and C: liver cancer.","HTLV-1: adult T-cell leukaemia.","HHV-8: Kaposi sarcoma.","H. pylori (a bacterium): gastric cancer and MALT lymphoma."],"HPV → cervix; EBV → Burkitt & nose; HBV/HCV → liver; HTLV-1 → T cells; HHV-8 → Kaposi."],
sh:"Oncogenic viruses: DNA viruses — HPV (16, 18: E6 inactivates p53, E7 inactivates RB; cervical, anogenital, oropharyngeal carcinoma), EBV (Burkitt lymphoma with t(8;14), nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, post-transplant lymphoma), HBV (chronic inflammation, regeneration, HBx protein; HCC), HHV-8 (Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma), Merkel cell polyomavirus. RNA viruses — HTLV-1 (Tax protein; adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma), HCV (chronic inflammation; HCC). Bacterium: H. pylori (gastric adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma).",
m:[["DNA oncogenic viruses",[["Virus","Mechanism","Cancer"],["HPV 16, 18","E6 → p53 degradation; E7 → RB inactivation; integration","Cervical, anal, penile, oropharyngeal ca"],["EBV","LMP-1 (mimics CD40), EBNA-2; B-cell proliferation","Burkitt, nasopharyngeal ca, Hodgkin, PTLD"],["HBV","Chronic inflammation, HBx protein, integration","Hepatocellular carcinoma"],["HHV-8","Viral cyclin, vFLIP","Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma"]]],
["RNA oncogenic viruses",["HTLV-1: Tax protein activates host genes → adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma (Japan, Caribbean)","HCV: chronic inflammation, regeneration → HCC"]],
["Bacterial","H. pylori: chronic gastritis → intestinal metaplasia → gastric adenocarcinoma; antigen-driven B cells → MALT lymphoma (regresses with eradication)."]],
l:{i:"About 15–20% of cancers worldwide are linked to infections; vaccination (HPV, HBV) prevents many of them.",x:[["Prevention",["HPV vaccine for girls 9–14 years","HBV vaccine at birth","H. pylori eradication"]]],f:"Oncogenic virus → viral oncoproteins (E6/E7, LMP-1, Tax) or chronic inflammation → p53/RB inactivation + proliferation → additional mutations → cancer",c:"Viruses contribute to cancer by disabling tumour suppressors or driving chronic regeneration; vaccines are a powerful preventive tool."}});
